"explanation": "The hallmarks of necrotizing fasciitis in this diabetic patient are severe pain out of proportion to initial appearance, rapidly spreading non-demarcated erythema, firm subcutaneous tissue, bullae filled with purple (hemorrhagic) fluid, high fever, and systemic toxicity. Answer F is correct because these features distinguish necrotizing fasciitis from simple cellulitis (A), which lacks skin breakdown, purple bullae, and firm subcutis. Erysipelas (C) has a sharply demarcated, raised, bright-red border without bullae or subcutaneous involvement. Treatment requires emergent surgical debridement.",
